<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zwiefalten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zwiefalten is a municipality in the district of Reutlingen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in 1089 by the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalm">Achalm</a> and settled from Hirsau, which became an imperial abbey in 1750. Its church, rebuilt from 1739 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-michael-fischer">Johann Michael Fischer</a> with stucco by Johann Michael Feichtmayr and frescoes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-spiegler">Franz Joseph Spiegler</a>, is a masterpiece of the late baroque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zwiefalten.jpg" alt="Zwiefalten" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagar66 based on work of TUBS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zwiefalten_in_RT.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zwiefalten lies in the district of Reutlingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. The name comes from its position in the valleys of the Zwiefalter Aach and the Kessel-Aach, and the place was first mentioned as Zwivaltum in a document of King Ludwig IV dated 15 June 904. The town's coat of arms shows two interlocking rings for the confluence of the two streams and seven golden stars taken from the arms of the former counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalm">Achalm</a>.</p><p>The municipality includes seven villages, one hamlet and three farmsteads, among them Baach, Gauingen, Sonderbuch and Upflamör, which were incorporated between 1938 and 1975. Nearby is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heuneburg">Heuneburg</a> of Upflamör, a Celtic rampart of 4.5 hectares in the forest that was settled no later than the 7th century BCE, and the Wimsener Höhle, a cave accessible by boat and known since 1447.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town owes its standing to the Benedictine abbey, founded in 1089 by monks from Hirsau. Counts Luitold von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalm">Achalm</a> and Kuno von Wülflingen made extensive donations to it. A convent had once stood on the spot, but by the 14th century it had gone. The monastery bought a large territory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> and stayed influential until the 15th century. In 1525, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, peasants looted it. In 1750 it received the status of an imperial abbey, an independent power answerable only to the Imperial Crown and free of Württemberg's rule.</p><p>The monastery was forcibly dissolved in the secularisation, and in 1812 the royal Württemberg sanatorium was established in its buildings. During the Nazi killing action known as T4, the state hospital at Zwiefalten served as an intermediate station for the killing centre at Grafeneck Castle. At least 1 673 mentally ill people of all ages were moved from Zwiefalten in 1939 and 1940. The first transport, of 50 women, left on 2 April 1940, and by 9 December more than 1,000 patients had been taken in 22 transports to Grafeneck. Killing by injection continued in Zwiefalten itself. In 1949 the former director Martha Fauser was sentenced to one year and six months for manslaughter, and a memorial stone in the cemetery recalls the victims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former abbey church, begun in 1739 and completed by 1765, is considered a masterpiece of the German late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. Its interior holds a late Gothic miraculous image of 1430, frescoed ceilings and an altarpiece by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-spiegler">Franz Joseph Spiegler</a>, frescoes by Andreas Meinrad von Au and stucco by Johann Michael Feuchtmayer. The former monastery now forms part of the psychiatric hospital, and the town lies on the B 312 between Reutlingen and Riedlingen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zwiefalten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Zwiefalten</b> was founded in 1089 by the counts of Achalm as a Benedictine monastery, settled from Hirsau.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-zwiefalten">Imperial Abbey of Zwiefalten</a></li></ul></section>
